Critique:
{
  "requires_revision": true,
  "summary": "The candidate decomposition is broadly aligned with the domain capabilities and avoids technical-layer boundaries, but it has several evidence-backed defects: it omits the reporting/export capability, omits supporting master data administration, omits the operations/continuity capability, and places vacation balance calculation inside the HR Service without acknowledging its dependency on employment agreement data. It also introduces an unsupported interaction from Document Service to User Management Service, and its interaction graph is incomplete for several evidenced cross-domain flows.",
  "issues": [
    {
      "category": "omitted_capability",
      "severity": "high",
      "description": "The candidate has no service responsible for reporting and export, even though the domain analysis identifies 'Reporting and export' as a distinct capability with evidence RF-63, RF-64, and RF-65. Reports span billing, hours, projects, proposals, interactions, organizations, personal use, and commissions, and must be exportable in CSV, PDF, HTML, RTF, XLS, and ODT.",
      "evidence": [
        "Domain analysis capability 'Reporting and export' with evidence RF-63, RF-64, RF-65",
        "Original evidence RF-63, RF-64, RF-65",
        "Static analysis shows com.autentia.tnt.report and com.autentia.tnt.bean.reports as a distinct reporting/export cluster"
      ],
      "recommendation": "Add a Reporting and Export Service responsible for cross-domain reports and multi-format exports, or explicitly assign this capability to an existing service with a clear rationale. If added, declare its interactions with the services whose data it reads."
    },
    {
      "category": "omitted_capability",
      "severity": "high",
      "description": "The candidate does not assign responsibility for supporting master data administration (departments, contract types, user categories, organization types, interaction types, tax reasons, billing schemes, frequencies) despite RF-01 and RF-ADM-04. These reference data are used across HR, CRM, billing, and user management, so leaving them unassigned creates a material gap.",
      "evidence": [
        "Domain analysis capability 'Supporting master data administration' with evidence RF-01, RF-ADM-04",
        "Original evidence RF-01 and RF-ADM-04",
        "Static analysis shows com.autentia.tnt.manager.admin and com.autentia.tnt.bean.admin as a distinct administrative cluster"
      ],
      "recommendation": "Either add a dedicated Master Data Administration Service or explicitly assign this capability to an existing service (e.g., User Management Service or a new Administration Service) and document how the reference data are shared."
    },
    {
      "category": "omitted_capability",
      "severity": "medium",
      "description": "The candidate omits the operations and continuity capability, including external storage for attachments and reports, startup validation of configuration directories/files, automatic preparation of upload storage structure, and configuration-driven enabling/disabling of operational rules (RNF-75 through RNF-78).",
      "evidence": [
        "Domain analysis capability 'Operations and continuity' with evidence RNF-75, RNF-76, RNF-77, RNF-78",
        "Original evidence RNF-75 through RNF-78"
      ],
      "recommendation": "Add an Operations and Continuity Service or explicitly assign these responsibilities to an existing service, such as Document Service for external storage and a platform/configuration service for startup validation and feature toggles."
    },
    {
      "category": "unsupported_interaction",
      "severity": "medium",
      "description": "The candidate declares that Document Service communicates with User Management Service, but the evidence does not show a direct interaction from document management to user management. The documented relationship is that documents are associated with responsible people and organizational areas, and that a dedicated document category is automatically created per user (RF-51, RF-52, RF-53). This is better modeled as User Management Service depending on Document Service, or as a shared reference, not as Document Service calling User Management Service.",
      "evidence": [
        "Original evidence RF-51, RF-52, RF-53",
        "Domain analysis capability 'Document management'",
        "Candidate Document Service communicates_with User Management Service"
      ],
      "recommendation": "Remove the Document Service -> User Management Service interaction or reverse it to User Management Service -> Document Service if the automatic per-user document category creation is implemented as a user-management-triggered operation."
    },
    {
      "category": "missing_interaction",
      "severity": "medium",
      "description": "The candidate does not declare interactions required by evidenced cross-domain flows. For example, Billing Service must import billable hours and project costs from Project Service (RF-42) and convert billable items from proposals (RF-43), but the candidate only shows Billing Service communicating with CRM Service and Project Service without specifying the direction or the proposal-conversion flow. Similarly, Order Service sends documents related to an order by email (RF-62), which requires an interaction with Notification Service or Document Service, but the candidate only shows Order Service -> Notification Service without covering document sending.",
      "evidence": [
        "Original evidence RF-42, RF-43, RF-62",
        "Domain analysis capabilities 'Billing and accounts receivable/payable management' and 'Order and commission management'",
        "Candidate interaction graph"
      ],
      "recommendation": "Add or clarify the missing interactions: Project Service -> Billing Service for billable hours and project costs; CRM Service -> Billing Service for proposal-to-billing conversion; Order Service -> Document Service or Notification Service for sending order-related documents by email."
    },
    {
      "category": "responsibility_overlap",
      "severity": "medium",
      "description": "The candidate places vacation balance calculation inside HR Service, but the domain analysis identifies 'Vacation balance calculation' as a distinct capability with business rules that depend on employment agreement data (annual workload, vacation allowance) and on approved vacation requests. This creates a weak cohesion risk because HR Service also manages employment agreements and vacation requests, and the calculation rules are complex enough to warrant explicit ownership.",
      "evidence": [
        "Domain analysis capabilities 'Employment agreement management', 'Vacation request management', and 'Vacation balance calculation'",
        "Original evidence RF-03, RF-11, RF-12, RF-13",
        "Candidate HR Service responsibility includes vacation balances"
      ],
      "recommendation": "Either keep vacation balance calculation in HR Service but explicitly document its dependency on employment agreement data and approved requests, or extract a Vacation Balance Service if the calculation rules are expected to evolve independently."
    },
    {
      "category": "missing_interaction",
      "severity": "low",
      "description": "The candidate does not declare an interaction between Authentication and Authorization Service and the services whose critical entities it protects. The domain analysis states that access control applies to documents, billing records, users, and other critical entities (RNF-69), which implies that Document Service, Billing Service, and User Management Service must consult or be enforced by the authorization service.",
